   "id": "g2-6",
   "name": "Factors of the uneven distribution of population"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Physical factors:\n• relief: lowlands and gentle slopes attract people; steep, high mountains repel them;\n• climate: mild climates with enough rain attract; very hot, very cold or very dry climates repel;\n• soils: fertile soils (river valleys, volcanic soils such as on Mount Cameroon) attract farmers;\n• water: rivers, lakes and springs attract people;\n• vegetation and diseases: dense forests and areas with tsetse flies or malaria repel.\nHuman factors:\n• economic activities: mines, industries, ports, plantations and trade attract people;\n• towns, roads and services (schools, hospitals);\n• history and politics: wars push people away; government policies can move people.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "p": "the Mount Cameroon area"
  },
  "problem": "Give two reasons why {p} is densely populated.",
  "steps": [
   "Physical: the volcanic soils are very fertile, good for farming.",
   "Human: plantations (tea, rubber, oil palm, bananas) and towns such as Buea and Limbe give jobs."
  ],
  "answer": "Fertile volcanic soils; plantations and towns give jobs.",
  "checks": []
